    "result_text": "All tests pass. The full suite (the 3 acceptance tests \u2014 the only test classes in the project) is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization for owner creation by adding a focused, single-responsibility component and wiring it into the one place owners are created.\n\n**New file \u2014 `AddressNormalizer.java`** (in `rest/validation`, alongside the existing `EmailNormalizer` and `TelephoneNormalizer`):\n- Trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands whole-word street-type abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`).\n- Returns an empty string for `null`/blank input so a required-field check can reject it.\n- Expansion is token-based (splits on spaces and maps each word), so it won't mangle substrings inside longer words.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`**: normalizes the address on the DTO as the very first step, before any validation:\n```java\nownerFieldsDto.setAddress(this.addressNormalizer.normalize(ownerFieldsDto.getAddress()));\nthis.ownerRequestValidator.validate(ownerFieldsDto);\nthis.householdDuplicateValidator.validate(ownerFieldsDto);\n```\n\nNormalizing up front means the change flows through the existing code without duplication:\n- **Required-field check**: `OwnerRequestValidator` already uses `StringUtils.hasText`, so a whitespace-only address (now `\"\"`) is rejected \u2014 no change needed there.\n- **Household duplicate detection** and the **shared household id** both read the address off the (now-normalized) DTO/owner via the existing `HouseholdKey`, so both comparisons use the normalized form automatically.\n- **Stored/returned value**: the mapper copies the normalized address onto the `Owner`, so it's persisted and returned normalized.\n\nNo existing logic was moved into a catch-all; the new behavior lives in its own normalizer and is composed exactly like the sibling email/telephone normalizers already were.",
